A||N||13||kinboyatuwo||Flames?||||5||
Z||000000||kinboyatuwo||11-02-2000||05:02 PM||||Okay i have seen the flames out the rear tail pife of classic cars and got a glimpse of them coming out the rear of a turbo RX7 (eeeewwwwww).  How and why does this happen?<P>Have heard that some are on purpose, a spark plug in the exaust or something like that.  Don't like the idea just kinda curious <P>Have seen them on a Lambo Diablo too?<P>Any info is great<BR>thanks<BR>from curious kinboyatuwo and the man in the yellow hat||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tigger||11-02-2000||05:23 PM||||Yep, they pull the choke, rev the engine, and light the plug at the end of the exhaust when the motor is winding down.<P>Personally, I think it's pretty cool.  You wouldn't have too many people wanting to ride your bumper if they seen 4 ft flames coming from your car.<P>I think you'd just about need an endless supply of rear bumper covers to do it on a Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000002||86SE||11-02-2000||05:45 PM||||My Fiero used to do this (before i started dismantaling the engine).  All I did was take out the cat converter.  I discovered the flames on accident.  I reved it up and heard a loud POP.  I thought that was pretty neat so I did it again and glanced out the rear of the car and saw a bright orange glow.  Then i poped the decklid, reved it from there and I was in the middle of 4 one and a half foot long flames (orange/blue - very hot!).<P>Then when I would race people, I would run it up to redline in 1st (at this time I am ahead of them) and put it into neutral.......POP!.....second....redline.....neutral......engine winds down to about 2500 rpm.......POP!.<P>It surprises the he!! outta them and is VERY LOUD.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||10
Z||000003||Old Lar||11-02-2000||06:22 PM||||The flame do look neat, but remember the rear fascia is plastic.  Plastic can melt/burn.  It is caused by a rich exhaust the still has unburned gas in it.  When it goes "pop", that is an explosion which could blow the muffler apart or crack welds or seams which fire can come out and burn something else under the car.  Just a precautionary warning.<P>The flames are a tailgating deterrent.||||reg||11
Z||000004||Formula||11-02-2000||09:09 PM||||LOL<P>I guess the combination of black smoke (running rich) and no cat must be the cause of that on fieros, I can control it though!<BR>If you run it up to like 4k in low it should shoot flames too.<P>not that I've ever done something like this||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||11-02-2000||10:21 PM||||Old Lar, if I am not mistake there are heat shields under each exhaust tip.  I could be wrong but I think I remember asking someone and thats what they told me they were.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||3
Z||000006||Standard||11-02-2000||10:23 PM||||My GT does that too.  No cat, but I don't get very long flames.  I think it's because my front exhaust manifold is almost cracked in half... sure is cool tho!  I used to always wonder why my car would pop so loud, almost sounded like it was backfiring.. then one day I was racing my friend in his Lumina and he said he saw some flames..  I don't think you really have to worry about melting the fascia, the flames don't stay for any period of time.  Anyways, there had to be a reason they put metal plates above the exhaust tips  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||prof bobo||11-02-2000||11:42 PM||||This reminds me of something _really funny_ that happened to me a long time ago with an old 1970 Ford LTD I had in college.  I started having problems with backfiring -idle was good, wide open it ran like a bat outta Hell, but just as you start to get on it it would stumble, hesitate, backfire and then take off.  Took me forever to figure out what was going on, turned out that the throttle cable would rub against the distributor wiring at a certain point and  over time had rubbed through and would short out the ignition when the gas pedal was at a specific position.  <P>Anyway, here's the funny part.  I'm driving down the highway with this problem in stop and go traffic.  I'd accelerate a little, the  ignition would short, the car would stumble and then take off as I hit the pedal some more.  Evidently it was annoying this guy behind me and he began to tailgate me.  At some point we had to slow down a little and the throttle hit that magic spot for a few seconds too long.  I guess the entire exhaust system filled with gas vapor because when it finally did spark again there was this tremendous *BOOM* and the car belched out the  largest cloud of black smoke I'd ever seen -it fully engulfed the tailgatin' guy.  (Yeah, my muffler was shredded).  I still remember looking in my rear view mirror and seeing that car emerge from the cloud with his headlights on, wipers going and eyes like saucers.  He kept about a 1/4 mile behind me after that.||||reg||1
Z||000008||BRIAN CORNELIUS||11-03-2000||08:36 AM||||For a great how to do it site try: <A HREF="http://www.kustoms.com/bbc/garage/techgar/exflam/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kustoms.com/bbc/garage/techgar/exflam/</A> <P>                        BRIAN||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||11-03-2000||08:50 PM||||On my 85 GT I have a slightly more aggressive cam so apparently I give a pretty good light show at night when I down shift. I never knew this until I was heading out to the freeway from work being followed by a bunch of friends in rice burners. I hit the on ramp first during some "spirited" driving and let out of the gas at around 4500 rpm going around the curve. When we got to the movies (where we were originally headed) the guy who had driven behind me was totally screaming to his friends about the big blue flames coming out of tail pipes.<P>There has been a VERY healthy respect for the Fiero among them ever since...<P>Still haven't seen my own flames but they sound impressive. I try not to do it any more because the POP I hear always make me nervous that the next pop will be a cylinder blowing out!||||reg||1
Z||000010||SibERThuG||11-04-2000||12:58 AM||||I put flames on mine and I hooked up a "grill" propane tank to it with a regulator, now I can shoot flames from 6" to 30 FEET! It cost about $40 to do, really cheap for it's outcome. It's a headturner, and peaople will always ask to see more! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Robbie<BR>88' Formula||||reg||11
